# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the one blank at the bottom.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# .env.local is ignored by git and must stay that way. This example file
# IS committed, and is the only record of what the app needs to run, so
# add a line here whenever you add a new setting — with a placeholder,
# never a real value.

# --- Supabase ---------------------------------------------------------
# These point at the LOCAL Supabase on your own machine, not the live
# one. That is deliberate. Local and live share nothing: a test signup
# here creates no real account, and a broken migration here cannot take
# the live site down.
#
# Start it first:  supabase start
# See docs/local-development.md if that is new to you.
#
# Both values below are identical on every machine that runs the local
# stack — they are baked into the Supabase CLI. They are not secret, do
# not change, and are safe to have in this committed file.
SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_ACJWlzQHlZjBrEguHvfOxg_3BJgxAaH

# The service-role key. Unlike the publishable key above, THIS ONE IS A
# REAL SECRET — it bypasses every RLS policy and can act as any user.
# Only used server-side (see lib/supabase/admin.ts), and only for the
# handful of things that genuinely need Supabase's admin API: banning
# and un-banning an account, which is what recoverable account deletion
# is built on (see supabase/migrations/20260902140000_*).
#
# For the local Docker stack: `supabase start` prints it (same fixed
# value on every machine, same as the publishable key above — copy it
# from there rather than trusting a copy pasted into a chat or doc).
# For a hosted project (dev or live): Project Settings -> API ->
# service_role, in the Supabase dashboard. Never commit a real value
# here — this file only ever gets a blank placeholder.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Where this copy of the site lives --------------------------------
# Used to build the link inside confirmation and password-reset emails.
#
# Locally that is localhost. On the live site it is the real address, and
# it is set in Vercel rather than here. If it is missing in production
# the app refuses to send those emails rather than quietly mailing people
# a link to their own computer.
SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Google Books -----------------------------------------------------
# The one value here you have to supply yourself, because there is no
# local version of Google.
#
# A key IS required: calling the API without one returns 429 with a quota
# of zero, whatever older guides say.
#
# To get one (free):
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com
#   2. Create a project, or pick an existing one
#   3. Enable the "Books API"
#   4. APIs & Services -> Credentials -> Create credentials -> API key
#
# Without it, book search says it is not set up rather than showing an
# empty list that would look like "no such book".
GOOGLE_BOOKS_API_KEY=

# --- Pointing at the live database instead ----------------------------
# Don't, unless you have a specific reason. If you must — say, to check
# something that only happens with real data — change the two Supabase
# values to the ones in Vercel, and change them back afterwards. While
# they are changed, everything you do goes to the live site.
